+# Extracting Tables from Images
+
+This example shows how to extract a structured table from an image by combining
+Instructor, a Zod response model, and a vision-capable OpenAI model.
+
+!!! tips "Motivation"
+
+    Many documents arrive as screenshots, scanned reports, or embedded images.
+    A structured response model gives the model a target shape for the title,
+    caption, and table data instead of returning free-form prose.
+
+## Define the table response
+
+Create a schema for the output you want to receive. The table field is kept
+flexible because image tables may have different columns and row shapes.
+
+```ts
+import Instructor from "@/instructor"
+import OpenAI from "openai"
+import { z } from "zod"
+
+const TableResponseSchema = z.object({
+  title: z.string(),
+  caption: z.string(),
+  table: z.any()
+})
+```
+
+## Configure Instructor
+
+Use Markdown JSON mode so the response can be parsed back into the schema.
+
+```ts
+const oai = new OpenAI({
+  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY,
+  organization: process.env.OPENAI_ORG_ID
+})
+
+const instructor = Instructor({
+  client: oai,
+  mode: "MD_JSON"
+})
+```
+
+## Extract the table
+
+Pass both the image and an instruction that asks for a concise title, caption,
+and table. The `response_model` tells Instructor which schema to validate.
+
+```ts
+const extractTableFromImage = async (imageUrl: string) => {
+  return instructor.chat.completions.create({
+    model: "gpt-4-vision-preview",
+    response_model: {
+      schema: TableResponseSchema,
+      name: "table extraction"
+    },
+    max_tokens: 1_000,
+    messages: [
+      {
+        role: "user",
+        content: [
+          {
+            type: "image_url",
+            image_url: { url: imageUrl }
+          },
+          {
+            type: "text",
+            text: `
+              First, reason about the best headers for the table.
+              Then write a concise title and caption.
+              Lastly, produce the table.
+            `
+          }
+        ]
+      }
+    ]
+  })
+}
+```
+
+## Run the example
+
+The full runnable example lives in
+`examples/extract-table-from-image/index.ts`.
+
+```ts
+const table = await extractTableFromImage(IMAGE_URL)
+console.log("Extracted Table: ", table)
+```
+
+!!! note "Schema design"
+
+    If your table format is known in advance, replace `z.any()` with a stricter
+    schema such as `z.array(z.object({ ... }))`. A stricter schema improves
+    validation and makes downstream processing safer.
